      "question": {
        ":question/id": "02afba5b-6298-5f27-a3bd-f58e8fe06b67",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{- \\sin{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}.\n$$\n\nThe negative derivative is included in the factor outside the new integral.\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{- \\sin{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\ln\\left|\\cos{\\left(2 x - \\frac{\\pi}{6} \\right)}\\right|\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
