      "question": {
        ":question/id": "e036cff3-c45f-5085-9516-dfa7e50ba67f",
        ":question/problem": "Evaluate exactly.\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3 \\cos^{3}{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=3 \\cos^{3}{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 5$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 36 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nThe negative derivative is included in the factor outside the new integral.\n\nChange both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=8\\\\x=\\frac{\\pi}{8}&\\Longrightarrow u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed bounds decrease; keep their order when subtracting endpoint values.\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\frac{7 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{3 \\cos^{3}{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{7}{36}\\left(\\int_{8}^{5} \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth new bounds are positive, so absolute values may be omitted. Evaluate:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{36}\\left(\\left[\\ln u\\right]_{8}^{5}\\right)=- \\frac{7}{36}\\left(\\ln\\left(5\\right)-\\ln\\left(8\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nUsing $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ gives\n\n$$\n- \\frac{7}{36}\\left(\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)\\right).\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
