        "title": "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "31af3e89-444a-5351-ab86-623f9917660d",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{- \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{field-1}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4 \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)}=u-2$, the remaining quotient is\n\n$$\n\\frac{u-2}{u}=1-\\frac{2}{u}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting, including the required constant factor, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int \\frac{- \\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} \\sec^{2}{\\left(4 x \\right)}}{\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int 1 - \\frac{2}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $u$:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\int 1 - \\frac{2}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(u-2\\left(\\ln\\left|u\\right|\\right)\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2-2\\left(\\ln\\left|\\tan{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2\\right|\\right)\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
